# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFY THE NAME SPLITS BEFORE COMMITTING THIS FILE.
#
# Every entry below was taken from the manuscript byline, which prints names in
# upper case without indicating which part is the family name. The splits here
# are a best guess and several are likely wrong. GitHub and Zenodo will render
# these publicly and citation managers will index them, so a wrong split
# produces a permanently miscited author.
#
# Ask each co-author how they want to be cited, then fix `family-names` and
# `given-names` accordingly. Add `orcid:` lines for anyone who has one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite both the software and the paper."
title: "MobileNetV3-TSM suspicious-activity detection on legacy CPU hardware"
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility package for a real-time suspicious-activity detection system
  built on MobileNetV3-Large with a Temporal Shift Module, trained on a curated
  binary subset of UCF-Crime and deployed to CPU-only legacy hardware through
  OpenVINO. Contains the experiment notebook, a standalone command-line training
  entry point, model and deployment configuration, and a deterministic
  manifest-generation script. UCF-Crime video files are not redistributed.
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/ikpeli/mobilenetv3-tsm-legacy-cpu-surveillance-"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21610681          # all-versions DOI; resolves to the latest release
version: 1.0.2
date-released: 2026-07-26
keywords:
  - video anomaly detection
  - suspicious activity recognition
  - temporal shift module
  - MobileNetV3
  - edge computing
  - CPU inference
  - OpenVINO
  - video surveillance
